    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\n> Move all the \"passive\" display stuff in the signs and numbers column to a separate `mini.signs` module and keep the \"active\" stuff from the virtual lines and work with hunks in `mini.diff`.\n> \n> I believe this keeps things more organized and even though they are related, they are different goals and it seems that this module does the work of two. It would also be easier to document.\n\nSo just to be clear, the proposed 'mini.signs' is only about showing 'mini.diff' data in the sign column?\n\nIf yes, then this is indeed very suboptimal. And I don't really agree with the \"does the work of two\" statement. 'mini.diff' has specific model of what it works with: each buffer can be assigned a reference text and the module is about interacting between current buffer text and the reference. Hunks are computed as the diff between current and reference text. There is a way to visualize this difference (via signs or colored line numbers). And there is a way to influence current text based on reference (reset) and reference text based on current (apply).\n\n> By the way (using gitsigns for now, but trying to keep everything possible in mini-nvim), I would like to have the signs column displaying the stage/buffer difference in detail (with number of lines removed) and the numbers column with colors indicating the working directory difference (or the state the file was in when it was opened)/buffer; Is this something in mini.diff?\n\nI am not 100% sure I understand the use case, but is the above information answers the question? I think the fact that there is a strong one-to-one relation between buffer and reference text probably suggests that the answer is \"No, at least not at the moment\".",
